CAT = "txadjLiability"; SEG = "LIA"
Q = [
("To prove negligence, a claimant must show a duty, a breach of that duty, damages and:",
 "Proximate cause", ["Intent to harm", "A written contract", "Strict liability"],
 "Negligence has four elements: a legal duty, breach of the duty, proximate cause linking the breach to the harm, and actual damages."),
("Lightning knocks out a home's power during a freeze, and the pipes later burst for lack of heat. Under proximate cause, which event is treated as causing the water loss?",
 "The lightning strike", ["The freezing weather", "The burst pipe itself", "The water damage"],
 "The proximate cause is the event that sets in motion an unbroken chain of events leading to the loss. Here that chain began with the lightning."),
("A company storing explosives is held liable for blast damage even though it used every precaution. This is:",
 "Strict liability", ["Vicarious liability", "Comparative negligence", "Contributory negligence"],
 "Strict (absolute) liability applies to ultrahazardous activities and some products regardless of fault or care taken."),
("An employer is held liable for a crash its delivery driver caused while on the job. This is:",
 "Vicarious liability", ["Strict liability", "Contractual liability", "Absolute liability"],
 "Vicarious liability makes one party answer for another's negligence because of their relationship, such as employer and employee acting in the course of the job."),
("Under Texas proportionate responsibility law, an injured claimant cannot recover damages if the claimant's share of responsibility is:",
 "Greater than 50 percent", ["Greater than 0 percent", "Equal to 50 percent", "Greater than 75 percent"],
 "Texas follows modified comparative responsibility (Civil Practice and Remedies Code Chapter 33): a claimant more than 50 percent responsible recovers nothing."),
("A Texas jury finds a claimant's damages are $80,000 and that the claimant was 30 percent responsible. The claimant recovers:",
 "$56,000", ["$80,000", "$24,000", "$40,000"],
 "Texas reduces recoverable damages by the claimant's percentage of responsibility: $80,000 x 70 percent = $56,000."),
("A rule that lets a negligent claimant recover when the defendant had the final opportunity to avoid the accident is:",
 "Last clear chance", ["Assumption of risk", "Res ipsa loquitur", "Attractive nuisance"],
 "Last clear chance holds the defendant liable if it could have avoided the harm after the claimant's negligence had already placed the claimant in danger."),
("A barrel falls from a warehouse window onto a passerby. Negligence may be inferred without direct proof under:",
 "Res ipsa loquitur", ["Last clear chance", "Assumption of risk", "Comparative fault"],
 "Res ipsa loquitur (the thing speaks for itself) allows an inference of negligence when the event would not normally happen without negligence and the defendant controlled the cause."),
("A property owner may owe extra care to trespassing children drawn onto the land by an unfenced pool under the doctrine of:",
 "Attractive nuisance", ["Last clear chance", "Assumption of risk", "Strict products liability"],
 "Attractive nuisance imposes a duty to protect children from dangerous conditions likely to attract them, even when they trespass."),
("A claimant's medical bills and lost wages are:",
 "Special damages", ["General damages", "Punitive damages", "Nominal damages"],
 "Special damages are measurable out-of-pocket losses. General damages compensate harms with no exact price, such as pain and suffering."),
("Compensation for a claimant's pain and suffering is classed as:",
 "General damages", ["Special damages", "Exemplary damages", "Liquidated damages"],
 "General damages compensate non-economic harm such as pain, suffering and disfigurement, which have no receipt or invoice."),
("Damages awarded to punish gross misconduct rather than to repay a loss are:",
 "Exemplary damages", ["Special damages", "General damages", "Liquidated damages"],
 "Exemplary (punitive) damages punish and deter. Texas law limits when and how much they may be awarded."),
("A thief pries open a locked back door after hours and takes inventory. In insurance terms this is:",
 "Burglary", ["Robbery", "Mysterious disappearance", "Embezzlement"],
 "Burglary is taking property from inside premises by someone who forcibly entered or exited, shown by visible marks. Robbery involves force or threat against a person."),
("Which term is the broadest, covering any act of stealing?",
 "Theft", ["Burglary", "Robbery", "Safe burglary"],
 "Theft is any act of stealing and includes burglary and robbery, which are narrower forms."),
("A ring is simply gone, and no one knows how or when it was lost. This is called:",
 "Mysterious disappearance", ["Burglary", "Robbery", "Conversion"],
 "Mysterious disappearance is the unexplained loss of property. Many theft coverages limit or exclude it."),
("If an insured signs a release freeing the at-fault party before settling with its own insurer, the insured may:",
 "Impair the insurer's subrogation", ["Increase the claim payment", "Speed up the appraisal", "Void the other driver's policy"],
 "Policies require the insured to do nothing after a loss to impair the insurer's right of recovery. A release can destroy that right and coverage with it."),
("Which settlement basis most directly supports the principle of indemnity?",
 "Actual cash value", ["Agreed value", "A valued policy law", "Stated amount"],
 "Indemnity restores the insured to the pre-loss position, no better and no worse. Actual cash value pays the property's worth at the time of loss."),
("In insurance terms, risk means:",
 "Uncertainty about a loss", ["The cause of a loss", "A condition raising loss odds", "The money paid on a claim"],
 "Risk is uncertainty concerning loss. The cause of a loss is a peril, and a condition that increases the chance or severity of loss is a hazard."),
("Which is a speculative risk that insurance generally will not cover?",
 "Buying stock that may fall", ["A warehouse that may burn", "A driver who may crash", "A roof that hail may hit"],
 "Speculative risk offers a chance of gain as well as loss. Insurance covers pure risk, where the only outcomes are loss or no loss."),
("Smoke damage to a store's walls from a fire next door is a:",
 "Direct loss", ["Indirect loss", "Consequential loss", "Time element loss"],
 "Physical damage to the insured property from a peril is a direct loss. Lost income while the store is closed would be an indirect loss."),
("A spectator who is hit by a foul ball at a ballpark may be barred from recovery under the traditional defense of:",
 "Assumption of risk", ["Last clear chance", "Res ipsa loquitur", "Vicarious liability"],
 "Assumption of risk applies when a person knowingly and voluntarily accepts a known danger. Many states, including Texas, now fold it into comparative responsibility."),
("A new, independent act that breaks the chain between the original negligence and the injury is a(n):",
 "Intervening cause", ["Proximate cause", "Contributory act", "Concurrent peril"],
 "An intervening (superseding) cause breaks the chain of causation, so the original negligence is no longer the proximate cause."),
("Under Texas law, a defendant is jointly and severally liable for all of a claimant's damages when the defendant's share of responsibility is:",
 "Greater than 50 percent", ["Greater than 10 percent", "Greater than 25 percent", "Equal to the claimant's"],
 "Texas Civil Practice and Remedies Code Section 33.013 makes a defendant more than 50 percent responsible jointly and severally liable. Others pay only their own share."),
("Under pure contributory negligence, a claimant who is even slightly at fault:",
 "Recovers nothing", ["Recovers in full", "Recovers half", "Recovers less their share"],
 "Pure contributory negligence bars any recovery by a claimant who contributed to the harm. Comparative negligence instead reduces the award by the claimant's share."),
]
